  data:  # f313
    kind: invariant  # f313
    title: "Kind-blind candidate selection credits a Go method call to a same-package function row: 9 entries mis-credited, and tr.Do on an http.Transport is reported as a net_send boundary that does not exist"  # f313
    status: violated  # f313
    priority: 2  # f313
    parent:  # f313
    tags:  # f313
    - go  # f313
    - io_boundary  # f313
    - analysis_quality  # f313
    - false_positive  # f313
    isbefore: []  # f313
    duplicate_of: []  # f313
    not_duplicate_of: []  # f313
    pr_ref:  # f313
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-11 while correcting the Go arm of measurement 0002. Reproduced\nend-to-end through the real `hypergumbo io-boundaries` CLI, not only at the\ncatalogue API.\n\nTHE DEFECT. When a short name exists in the catalogue as BOTH a package-level\nfunction row and a type-method row, the module filter keeps both (the package\nhint matches both by the component rule) and selection falls through to\ndeclaration order. The function row is declared first in go.yaml every time, so a\ngenuine method call is credited to the package-level function.\n\n  lookup_with_module('Get',  'net/http',  cc='method') -> net/http.Get   [FUNCTION]\n  lookup_with_module('Info', 'log/slog',  cc='method') -> log/slog.Info  [FUNCTION]\n\nMEASURED POPULATION: 9 of go.yaml's 45 method entries —\n  net/http.Client.{Get, Post, Head}\n  log/slog.Logger.{Debug, Info, Warn, Error, Log}\n  testing.B.TempDir\n\nPRODUCTION CONSEQUENCE, confirmed live:\n  func benchTempDir(b *testing.B) { b.TempDir() }   -> credited testing.T.TempDir\n  tr := &http.Transport{}; tr.Do(nil)               -> credited net/http.Client.Do\n  func clientGet(c *http.Client) { c.Get(u) }       -> credited net/http.Get\nThe middle one is a FALSE POSITIVE, not a mislabel: http.Transport has no Do\nmethod, so a net_send boundary is reported for a call that does not exist.\n\nIT INVERTS SIGN BETWEEN INSTRUMENT AND PRODUCT, which is the part most likely to\nbe miscounted later. The catalogue-reach instrument scores these as FALSE\nNEGATIVES (it matches on qualified name, and the name it wanted did not come\nback). Production manifests them as FALSE POSITIVES (a chain exists, under the\nwrong primitive). Any dead-code or FP-reduction accounting that consumes the reach\nnumber will mis-sign all 9.\n\n*** THREE FIXES ARE ALREADY REFUTED. Do not spend a session rediscovering them. ***\n\n(a) KEY ON call_construct. Refuted: Go stamps it \"method\" on every\n    selector-expression call including package-qualified free functions, so\n    `http.Get(u)` and `c.Get(u)` are byte-identical in (name, module_hint,\n    call_construct). See the sibling item on that vocabulary.\n\n(b) CARRY THE RECEIVER TYPE INTO THE MODULE HINT (type-granular instead of\n    package-granular). Refuted by direct measurement: fixes 1 of 9.\n      lookup('Get',  'net/http.Client',  cc='method') -> net/http.Get   STILL WRONG\n      lookup('Post', 'net/http.Client',  cc='method') -> net/http.Post  STILL WRONG\n      lookup('Debug','log/slog.Logger',  cc='method') -> log/slog.Debug STILL WRONG\n      lookup('TempDir','testing.B',      cc='method') -> testing.B.TempDir  FIXED\n    Cause: _module_matches('net/http', 'net/http.Client') is True by the\n    component rule, so the function row survives the filter no matter how granular\n    the hint is, and declaration order still decides. Only testing.B is fixed,\n    because _module_matches('testing.T','testing.B') is False.\n\n(c) PREFER THE method-KIND ROW GLOBALLY. Refuted as a deletion channel and by\n    corpus census. As a FILTER it removes 154 of go.yaml's 162 function rows from\n    reach and deletes 186 of 195 (95.4%) of alertmanager's real catalogued\n    boundary calls. And no global preference can be right in either direction:\n    prometheus has 43 package-level http.Get/Post/Head calls and 0 receiver-form;\n    alertmanager has 0 package-level slog calls and 97 receiver-form. The same\n    lookup triple arrives from both. Whichever way a preference points it is\n    wrong on one of the two repos.\n    (The 95.4% and the census come from an adversarial review pass, not from my\n    own run. Re-measure before relying on the exact figures; the DIRECTION is what\n    matters and it is not in doubt.)\n\nWHAT THE PAYOFF ACTUALLY IS, so the risk is priced honestly: every reachable\nkind-ambiguous pair reportedly carries the SAME boundary value, so correcting the\nselection changes the qualified_name string in the report and nothing else — no\nboundary class, zone, HIGH_RISK flag or taint verdict moves. That makes this a\nLABEL defect for 8 of the 9, and a real false positive for the Transport.Do shape.\nWeigh accordingly before spending a deletion budget on it.\n\nTHE ADMISSIBLE FIX, therefore, is real receiver-TYPE evidence with EXACT\ncomparison, not a preference and not a looser hint — the shape Python already uses\nagainst the sqlalchemy `conn: Connection` false positive. Not built, not sized.\n\nNEGATIVE CONTROL REQUIRED BEFORE ANY TYPE-GRANULAR GO SLOT SHIPS: assert that a\nhint equal to a bare catalogue leaf type (Client, Conn, Context, Cmd, Server,\nLogger, T, B) matches ZERO catalogue rows. It reportedly matches 51 today via\n_module_matches's suffix arm — verify that number first, then hold it at zero."  # f313
    fields:  # f313
      statement: "When a short name is catalogued as both a package-level function and a type method, selection must not fall through to declaration order."  # f313
      root_cause: "The module filter keeps both rows because the package-granular hint matches both by the component rule, and selection then returns candidates[0] on go.yaml declaration order, where the function row is always declared first."  # f313
